    <!-- Prompts -->
    <string name="cb_review_up_title">Ready for the next stage?</string>
    <string name="cb_review_up_text">This week %1$d of %2$d logged days stayed within your rule, and the mornings were never worse. Next: %3$s. Move up, or stay another week? Both are fine.</string>
    <string name="cb_review_up_check">Before limit climbing it\'s best to have it looked at again.</string>
    <string name="cb_move_up">Move up</string>
    <string name="cb_stay_week">Stay this week</string>
    <string name="cb_review_repeat_title">Stay at this stage for now</string>
    <string name="cb_review_repeat_text">On %1$s you logged %2$d of 10, above your rule of %3$d. The usual approach is to repeat the week. You can still change the stage yourself.</string>
    <string name="cb_review_repeat_text_generic">Some days were above your rule this week. The usual approach is to repeat the week. You can still change the stage yourself.</string>
    <string name="cb_repeat_week">Repeat week</string>
    <string name="cb_review_back_title">Your pain has gone up</string>
    <string name="cb_review_back_text">This week had days clearly above your rule. That usually means the load is too much right now. Suggestion: go back one stage for 3–4 days. If it keeps rising, or you notice swelling, numbness or a new pop, get it checked.</string>
    <string name="cb_step_back">Step back one stage</string>
    <string name="cb_keep_stage">Keep my stage</string>
    <string name="cb_review_stay_text">Not enough entries this week to say more – keep logging and stay at your stage.</string>
    <string name="cb_worse_title">Your pain has gone up</string>
    <string name="cb_worse_text">Over the last days your morning value went from %1$d to %2$d. That usually means the load is too much right now. Suggestion: go back one stage for 3–4 days. If it keeps rising, or you notice swelling, numbness or a new pop, get it checked.</string>
    <string name="cb_nb14_title">Two weeks – the pain hasn\'t gone down</string>
    <string name="cb_nb14_text">Your entries are about where they were at the start. Most strains feel better after about two weeks, so this is a good time to have it looked at – a doctor or physio can check whether something else is going on. Meanwhile you can stay at your stage or step back one.</string>
    <string name="cb_nb21_title">Three weeks without improvement: please get it checked</string>
    <string name="cb_nb21_text">Your pain has stayed about the same for three weeks. An ultrasound can show what\'s going on, and the plan may need to change.</string>
